{"decision":"FIX","summary":"The approach is a plausible general MVP improvement and preserves retrieval candidates, but the evidence labels overstate what the catalog explicitly proves. That can make the reranker confidently avoid valid sparse-catalog candidates, undermining the stated advisory/fail-open safety goal.","issues":["`compatibility_summary` labels heuristic defaults as `satisfied` catalog evidence: `candidate_valve_type()` treats absence of special keywords as `standard`, and `candidate_control()` defaults every ball valve to `manual`. These are useful inferences, not explicitly confirmed catalog facts.","A missing requested designation is always labeled `contradictory`, even when the candidate has no identifiable designation in its article/name/series fields. This conflicts with the prompt contract that unavailable catalog attributes are `unverified`, and may suppress a valid candidate due to incomplete metadata.","The new tests validate evidence rendering and candidate-list preservation, but do not cover the important sparse-data distinction above. Thus they do not adequately test the reliability claim behind the hypothesis."],"fix_plan":["Refactor compatibility evidence to distinguish explicit catalog confirmation/contradiction from heuristic inference; emit unavailable or inference-only attributes as `unverified` (or a clearly separate advisory category), not `satisfied`/`contradictory`.","For requested designations, mark a candidate contradictory only when a comparable documented designation is present and differs; otherwise mark it unverified.","Add focused tests for generic/sparse ball-valve records, inferred standard/manual values, and absent candidate designations, then rerun the public measurement before promotion."],"next_direction":"Compatibility evidence remains a credible reranking direction, but its value depends on conservative evidence provenance: explicit metadata contradictions are high-signal; inferred defaults and absent fields must not be presented as hard catalog facts."}
